---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj#article',
  headline: 'exFAT vs FAT32 for CDJs - Which Format Actually Works',
  description: 'Which format works on CDJ-2000, CDJ-3000 and XDJ players? The honest compatibility table, the 4GB myth, and the one setup that works everywhere.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj',
};
---
<Base
  title="exFAT vs FAT32 for CDJ USB Drives - The Real Answer | Save My Gig!"
  description="Which format works on CDJ-2000, CDJ-3000 and XDJ players? The honest compatibility table, the 4GB myth, and the one setup that works everywhere."
  path="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj"
  ogImage="/og/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line">Format_Question</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">exFAT vs <span class="accent">FAT32</span>:<br>WHICH ONE FOR CDJs?</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>The answer that never fails:</strong> FAT32 with an MBR partition map plays on every Rekordbox-compatible player ever made. exFAT works only on newer hardware. If you play in booths you don't control, FAT32 is the professional choice.</p>
    </div>

        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Compatibility, honestly</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><span class="mono">FAT32 + MBR</span>, works on CDJ-850, 900, 2000, 2000NXS/NXS2, 3000, every XDJ. The universal key.</li>
      <!-- Scope corrected against AlphaTheta's official exFAT FAQ: it names
           CDJ-3000, XDJ-RX3 and XDJ-XZ (firmware thresholds in facts.js), and
           the CDJ-3000X and XDJ-AZ product FAQs add both to the list. The old
           line said "CDJ-3000 and recent XDJ firmware", which was both too
           narrow and too vague, and omitted the NXS2. -->
      <li><span class="mono">exFAT</span>, supported on CDJ-3000, CDJ-3000X, CDJ-1500X, XDJ-RX3, XDJ-XZ and XDJ-AZ (firmware matters, see the <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox#which-player">player table</a>); <strong>refused by every CDJ-2000, including the NXS2</strong>, still common in clubs worldwide. One trap almost nobody mentions: rekordbox writes exFAT to <strong>USB drives only</strong>. It will not export to an exFAT <strong>SD card</strong> at all, whatever the player supports.</li>
      <li><span class="mono">NTFS / APFS</span>, never. AlphaTheta's own rekordbox manual lists NTFS as unsupported on both SD cards and USB drives, so rekordbox will not export to it in the first place.</li>
      <li><span class="mono">Partition scheme</span>, choose <span class="mono">MBR</span>. AlphaTheta documents file systems but not partition schemes, so treat this one as our recommendation rather than a published requirement. A drive left on GPT, which is what you get if you erase the volume instead of the device on a Mac, is a recurring cause of "it formatted fine but the player refuses it". MBR costs you nothing.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">"But FAT32 has a 4GB limit!"</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p>Per file, not per drive. No DJ track approaches 4GB; even a 30-minute WAV is under 1GB. The only DJs who genuinely need exFAT are the ones carrying multi-hour uncompressed recordings on their gig drive, which is its own mistake. For music libraries, the limit is irrelevant.</p>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The trap: how drives ship</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Most drives above 32GB ship <strong>exFAT from the factory</strong>, so a brand-new "professional" 64GB stick fails on a CDJ-2000 out of the box.</li>
      <li>Windows won't offer FAT32 for large drives in the Format dialog; on Mac, choose <span class="mono">MS-DOS (FAT)</span> in Disk Utility with <span class="mono">Master Boot Record</span> - <a href="/fix/format-usb-for-cdj">full walkthrough here</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">The rule to remember</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <p><strong>Format for the oldest player you might ever face, not the most modern one you own.</strong> The festival main stage has CDJ-3000s; the afterparty basement has a CDJ-2000 with a cigarette burn. FAT32 plays both.</p>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="exfat_vs_fat32" />
  </main>
</Base>
